// ORDER DEPENDENCE -- read this before editing.
//
// This fixture depends on the executor's ready queue being FIFO round-robin
// (`crates/nova-runtime/src/task.rs:184`): a task re-queued by `yield_now`
// waits behind every task already waiting. That is a documented runtime
// invariant, not an observed coincidence, but it is what makes the
// interleaving below deterministic. The output order IS the assertion here,
// so an edit that reorders the two `spawn`s, or adds a suspension point to
// either task, changes what this pins.
//
// WHAT THIS PINS: `send` does not suspend when the channel has room. Its
// `yield_now().await` belongs in the retry path and nowhere else, so a `send`
// that can enqueue immediately must do so without yielding. The channel is
// fresh and has capacity 2, so `produce`'s first `push` succeeds and its
// retry loop is never entered at all.
//
// This is `send`'s half of what `channel_recv_suspends_only_to_retry` pins
// for `recv`, and it exists because neither `channel_two_tasks_blocking` nor
// that fixture can see it: moving `send`'s `yield_now().await` out of the
// retry loop to before the first `push` leaves both of them green. Measured,
// not assumed. Same reasoning as the `recv` fixture for why the placement is
// pinned from the terminating side rather than asserted directly -- a retry
// loop with no suspension point livelocks rather than answering wrongly, and
// a fixture that hangs on regression is worse than none.
//
// NO TERMINATION GUARD HERE, unlike the `recv` twin, which closes its channel
// so that a regressed `pop` produces a bounded diff. This fixture never closes
// anything, so if `push` ever regressed to refusing every value its `send`
// would spin forever and this fixture would HANG rather than fail -- measured,
// a timeout kill with no output. Closing would not fix it: a closed channel
// makes `send` return false immediately and the fixture would pin nothing, and
// a third task to close it would add the very suspension point this
// interleaving depends on the absence of. Documented, not fixed; see the
// `close` comment in `channel_recv_suspends_only_to_retry.nova`.
async fn produce(mut tx: Sender<Int>) {
    let sent = tx.send(7).await
    println("send ${sent}")
}

// A second runnable task, so that a suspension inside `produce` is
// observable as output order rather than as a timing difference nothing can
// assert on.
async fn marker() {
    println("marker")
}

async fn main() {
    let ch: Channel<Int> = channel(2)
    let a = spawn(produce(ch.sender()))
    let b = spawn(marker())
    a.join().await
    b.join().await
}
